WEBVTT

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:20.580
Welcome to NASA Launchpad.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.160
I'm your host, Amber Whalen.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.120
Do you remember when we talked about the solid rocket boosters,

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.380
or SRBs, on the space shuttle, and we left you on a cliffhanger

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.840
just two minutes into flight?

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.580
Well, you don't have to wait any longer because we're going to dive into the SSMEs and external

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.380
tank right here, right now on NASA Launchpad.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.340
Okay, first thing first, what is an SSME?

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.700
Well SSME stands for Space Shuttle Main Engine.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.620
So if you're looking at the back of the Space Shuttle Orbiter, those three engine nozzles

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:53.500
you see there are attached to or actually a part of the main engines, the SSMEs.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.650
Now let's do a little comparing and contrasting with the SRBs, shall we?

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:59.960
The SRBs are separate rockets that

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.950
are attached to the shuttle system and are jettisoned during the flight, while the SSMEs

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:07.390
are built into the shuttle orbiter.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.970
The SRBs are much more powerful than the SSMEs.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:17.490
They provide 71% of the thrust from liftoff to SRB separation, up to 3.1 million pounds

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:17.890
of thrust.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:21.150
Each shuttle main engine has only 400,000 pounds of thrust.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:26.980
So the solid fuel in the SRB is generally more powerful, but it does have its disadvantages.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.820
For one thing, it's virtually impossible to adjust the

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.320
amount of energy given off by the solid fuel once it's ignited.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.770
But with liquid propellant, like in a car, it's easier to control how much fuel is going

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:41.620
into the engine, which in turn lets you control how fast the vehicle goes.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:47.530
But unlike a car, the SSMEs don't use regular unleaded or even diesel fuel.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:52.330
They actually burn liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen, which are supplied by the space shuttle's

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:53.010
external tank.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.010
That's the big orange part of the shuttle transportation system.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.550
And I do mean big.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.970
The space shuttle engines collectively burn

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.660
burned 3,917 liters of liquid propellant per second.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.260
Just to put that into perspective,

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.280
if the shuttle engines burned water

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.380
instead of liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen,

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.260
the amount of fuel that could fill

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.520
an average-sized swimming pool

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.700
would be drained in only 25 seconds.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.680
The space shuttle engines are designed

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.380
to perform in extreme temperatures.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.700
When icy cold liquid hydrogen is burned with liquid oxygen,

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.800
the temperature inside the combustion chamber

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.520
reaches an incredible 3,300 degrees Celsius.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.800
That's higher than the boiling point of iron.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:29.970
No wonder the three

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.100
2.2 ton engines are taken to the Space Shuttle Main Engine Processing Facility after every

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.600
flight and inspected to see if any parts need to be replaced before the next mission.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.630
And just how powerful are hydrogen and oxygen as fuel sources?

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.560
Well, here with the demonstration is Dr. Richard Biles of the Virginia Air and Space Center.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.200
And do I really have to say it?

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:50.630
Don't try this at home.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:55.510
So now we have a hydrogen balloon, that's the balloon we filled up with a little bit

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:58.070
of oxygen and the rest hydrogen.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:02:59.960
And hydrogen is a lighter...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.600
than air gas and is one of the gases that makes the shuttle go and its main tank is

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.860
full of liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen and that's because they're really compressed.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.960
So when they meet together down at the bottom they fire.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:21.070
So I just wanted to show you what hydrogen does when you light it.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.010
Big boom, right?

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.760
Actually, rockets are just controlled explosions.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.970
So while our little demonstration made

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.600
not be exactly what happens with the shuttle, it does show you the kind of energy that hydrogen

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:35.880
is capable of generating.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:40.850
If a little balloon filled with hydrogen has that kind of energy, imagine how much potential

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:46.870
energy is stored in the massive 550,000 liter or 146,000 gallon external tank.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.440
But obviously, all that energy isn't released at one time.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:54.520
It's gradually released through a series of vents and valves, allowing for controlled

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.840
explosions, letting NASA control how fast the shuttle travels.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.980
Now, you know that the SRB

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.320
after their fuel is expended, separate from the shuttle and fall back down into the ocean.

00:04:04.828 --> 00:04:06.668
where they are retrieved for reuse.

00:04:06.876 --> 00:04:09.876
But the external tank stays with the shuttle until it's almost in orbit.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.520
so it has to come back down through the atmosphere at a high speed.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.820
This causes it to break apart so it's unable to be reused.

00:04:16.892 --> 00:04:20.732
However, the external tank technology is being reused in a different sense.

00:04:21.212 --> 00:04:24.412
You know that the space shuttle program is scheduled to end in 2010.

00:04:24.636 --> 00:04:27.676
and the shuttles, which served as a workhorse for the space agency.

00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:28.868
will be retired.

00:04:28.992 --> 00:04:29.992
And what's coming next?

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:31.940
That's right, the Constellation program.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.440
If you look closely at the Ares V rocket,

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:36.620
you might notice something very familiar.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.200
That big orange section right there in the middle?

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.630
Yeah, that's the core propulsion stage,

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:43.830
which is, as you can probably guess,

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:44.910
derived from the space ship.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:45.800
Settle's external tank.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.440
Sure, it's been modified, but isn't that what it's all about?

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:53.440
Learning from experience of the past and putting that knowledge to good use in the present

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:53.860
and future?

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.940
Sounds like a good idea to me anyway.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.200
Alright, that's all the time we've got for now.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:04:59.980
Until next time, I'm Amber Wa-

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.800
I'm Kaylin, thanks for watching NASA Launch Pad.
